# Generated TLS material — a lab CA, its wildcard certificate, the private keys
# and the PKCS#12 truststore, plus the truststore password file. None of it may
# ever enter the tracked tree (FR-053, FR-076, FR-085).
.certs/

# OpenSSL's CA serial file, written by `-CAcreateserial` EVERY time a leaf
# certificate is signed — which is every `certs` checkpoint of every setup.
#
# Same hazard as .gitops-remote/ below, and it would have sunk a reproducibility
# run on its own: pass 1 signs a certificate, .srl changes, the working tree is
# dirty from then on, and pass 2's `images` checkpoint correctly refuses to
# publish a -dirty tag (FR-017). A tracked file that a run rewrites makes the
# NEXT run unreproducible.
.srl

# Executed-run output: conformance surefire captures, benchmark reports, drill
# transcripts, timing records. Produced by running the lab, never committed —
# results describe one machine's one run (FR-085).
results/

# The local bare repository Argo CD reconciles from. Generated by gitops.sh and
# REMOVED BY cleanup.sh — which is exactly why it must not be tracked.
#
# It was tracked, and the consequence was not cosmetic. `make cleanup` deletes
# it, so every cleanup left 24 tracked files showing as deleted; `git status
# --porcelain` was therefore never empty; the revision-derived image tag gained
# a `-dirty` suffix; and `images.sh publish` correctly refused to publish an
# unreproducible tag (FR-017). The registry then stayed empty and the first
# visible symptom was ImagePullBackOff, three checkpoints later.
#
# In other words: the lab dirtied its own repository during cleanup and then
# refused to build because the repository was dirty.
.gitops-remote/

# Kubernetes/k8s ignore patterns, per the project-setup verification list.
*.secret.yaml
secrets/
.kube/
kubeconfig*
*.key
*.crt
*.pem
*.p12

# Universal
.DS_Store
*.tmp
*.swp
